WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.930
- [Nahre] We'd like to thank
Audible for supporting PBS.

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:05.220
- [LA] How we access
music is always evolving.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.440
From vinyl records to
cassette tapes to streaming.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.710
But a streaming service like
Spotify isn't just changing

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.080
how we access music,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.303
it's actually changing the music itself.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.790
Spotify has been upending
the music industry

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:20.190
for a solid decade.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.230
100 million subscribers have some

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.920
35 million songs on their
fingertips for $10 a month.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.090
This new business model
has completely changed

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:29.450
the way musicians make money.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:31.817
As New York Times music
critic Jon Caramanica put it,

00:00:31.817 --> 00:00:33.390
"I think it's probably easier than ever

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.547
to make a little bit of money.

00:00:35.547 --> 00:00:36.610
I think it's harder than
ever to make a lot of money."

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.370
- But the change isn't only economic.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.238
Bands now need to vie For a
coveted spot on one of Spotify's

00:00:41.238 --> 00:00:44.160
curated playlist to reach
the millions of listeners

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.500
who tune into these playlists every week.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.363
- When I saw "Capsize"
made New Music Friday,

00:00:49.363 --> 00:00:51.967
we were up in a hotel, and I was running

00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:55.370
around the hotel, like screaming and--

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:56.620
- He came and got me, yeah.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.200
- I felt like we made it,
like we won the lottery.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.470
- Does Spotify influence your music?

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:05.230
- I think we'd love to
say it doesn't (laughing)

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:09.230
but, at least I think,
it's in my head, probably,

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:12.178
and it's a weird thing to think about,

00:01:12.178 --> 00:01:14.090
like there's this trick
of sliding choruses

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:16.550
up to the beginning of songs

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.270
just to catch somebody on a playlist,

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.510
'cause a lot of those listeners
are just passive listeners,

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.580
they're not your followers.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:23.980
You're competing with
so much noise per week,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.550
so you gotta do something that
grabs people right up front.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.650
- This competition is
actually pushing some artists

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.160
to change how they write songs

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.050
in order to optimize
their music for Spotify.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.500
That might sound like the
music world is selling out,

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.360
but is it really anything new?

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.916
Not really.

00:01:39.916 --> 00:01:41.910
- Format has always changed
the way artists make music.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.330
In the early 20th century,
the poor sound quality

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.710
of the phonograph influenced
some early jazz bands

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.840
to replace their double bass with a tuba,

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.081
so listeners could actually
hear the instrument.

00:01:51.081 --> 00:01:54.600
("Black Horse Stomp")

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.660
In the 1920s, Russian
composer Igor Stravinsky

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.270
composed each of the four movements

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.100
in his "Serenade in A"
to be three minutes long,

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.690
so each could fit on one
side of the 78 RPM record.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.330
Soon, blues and country
musicians found themselves

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.370
trimming down songs to
fit on records, too.

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:12.150
It's a trend that would
extend for decades,

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.890
well into the FM radio era.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.097
Mark Katz, author of "Capturing Sound:

00:02:16.097 --> 00:02:17.880
"How Technology has Changed Music,"

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.287
told Smithsonian Magazine,

00:02:19.287 --> 00:02:21.290
"The three-minute pop song is basically

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.110
an invention of the phonograph."

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.300
So, Spotify is just a new phonograph.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.360
- To understand how
Spotify is changing music,

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.840
you have to follow the money.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.680
Spotify pays copyright holders

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:35.170
about $0.006 to $0.0084 per stream.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.340
But only if the listener streams the song

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.100
for at least 30 seconds.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.020
- If they don't stick around
for at least 30 seconds,

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.944
the artists don't get paid.

00:02:42.944 --> 00:02:44.060
(wrong answer tone buzzes)

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:47.050
- This has created a few
key incentives for artists.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.710
One is to put more songs on each album.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.560
More songs means more
opportunities to make money,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.470
and you can see this in recent albums

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.620
like Migos' "Culture II"
and Drake's "Scorpion,"

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:59.860
each of which packed a couple dozen songs.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.380
- More songs could also
help an album go gold.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:05.730
In 2016, the Recording
Industry Association of America

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.840
started factoring streams into the method

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.970
of certifying gold and platinum albums.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:11.590
So with more songs per album,

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.260
artists can increase their
chances of getting more streams,

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.270
and possibly see their album go gold.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.900
(cymbals tinkling)

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.930
- But while albums
might be getting longer,

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:21.940
pop songs are actually getting shorter.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.690
In 2013, the average
song on the Billboard 100

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.650
was three minutes and 50 seconds,

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.900
according to a recent report from Quartz.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.010
In 2018, that average dipped

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.600
to three minutes and 30 seconds.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.140
What does it have to do with Spotify?

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:38.160
The answer could be related
to Spotify's 30 second rule.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.600
Because the platform pays out

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.280
when a stream lasts 30 seconds,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.760
there's little financial incentive

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.800
to write super long songs.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.880
- There is, however, an incentive
to hook listeners quickly.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.300
Gone are the days of elaborate intros.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:51.610
Now, you're more likely to hear

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.610
artists frontload a song with choruses,

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.910
or go for broke in the first
few seconds of the song.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.210
Like in Katy Perry's "Swish Swish,"

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:00.758
which opens with a familiar
sample from Fatboy Slim.

00:04:00.758 --> 00:04:02.963
♪ They know what is what ♪

00:04:02.963 --> 00:04:04.770
♪ But they don't know what is what ♪

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.590
In 2017, singer-songwriter Emily Warren

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.777
told Pitchfork that session musicians,

00:04:09.777 --> 00:04:12.560
"Have generally been saying, 'oh, we need

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:13.760
"'to make something that
sounds like Spotify.'"

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.787
- So, what is the Spotify sound?

00:04:15.787 --> 00:04:19.070
"Lean On" by Major Lazer
and DJ Snake, featuring MØ,

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:20.380
is a pretty good example.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.660
With more than one billion
listeners on Spotify,

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.780
the 2015 hit was once the
platform's most streamed song.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.370
But what made it so
optimized for streaming?

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:32.220
For one, the song opens with
a catchy syncopated statement

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.220
of the main chords,

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.563
before quickly getting into MØ's vocals.

00:04:40.374 --> 00:04:42.095
♪ Do you recall ♪

00:04:42.095 --> 00:04:42.940
♪ That long ago ♪

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.720
The rest of "Lean On" contains elements

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.370
you're likely to find in many
successful songs on Spotify.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:50.968
It's got a danceable
four-on-the-floor beat.

00:04:50.968 --> 00:04:53.630
("Lean On")

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:55.753
It's got a simple and catchy chorus.

00:04:55.753 --> 00:04:57.789
♪ Blow a kiss, fire a gun ♪

00:04:57.789 --> 00:05:00.091
♪ All we need is somebody to lean on ♪

00:05:00.091 --> 00:05:01.150
It's under three minutes long,

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:02.760
and it's also clearly influenced

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.350
by streaming music's
first signature genre,

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.680
tropical house, which is a lighter take

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.177
on the deep house genre.

00:05:09.177 --> 00:05:10.960
(tropical beat)

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:14.628
Trop house exploded in popularity
on Spotify around 2015,

00:05:14.628 --> 00:05:17.440
bringing tropical sounding synthesizers

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.477
and dancehall elements
to a mainstream audience.

00:05:20.477 --> 00:05:22.530
"Lean On" also features one of pop music's

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:25.200
recently favorite
conventions, the pop drop,

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.270
which is essentially a toned-down version

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.103
of an EDM bass drop.

00:05:29.103 --> 00:05:31.867
♪ All we need is somebody to lean on ♪

00:05:31.867 --> 00:05:33.940
- "Lean On" is an
expertly crafted pop song

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.840
that's broadly appealing and familiar.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:37.130
Those are good qualities to have,

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:39.380
if your goal is to succeed on Spotify.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:41.940
After all, studies have
shown that people prefer

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.320
music that they're familiar with,

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.480
and streaming data
indicates that people tend

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.550
not to skip songs that
they've heard before.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.260
Many listeners today engage

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.927
in what digital marketers have dubbed,

00:05:50.927 --> 00:05:52.150
"lean-back listening."

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:53.870
This term describes Spotify listeners

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.570
who prefer to select a
playlist and let it roll,

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:58.620
rather than selecting songs individually.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.750
Music that is familiar and accessible

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.790
is more likely to blend
in with a playlist,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:04.340
and avoid getting skipped.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:07.580
Offset, of Migos, said he's
obsessed with industry data,

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.100
and uses it to modify how
he approaches writing music.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.510
But does tailoring your art
for the aggregate preferences

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:13.510
of a streaming service

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:15.500
make songwriting any less creative?

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.100
Grammy award winner Sarah
McLachlan seems to think so.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.307
She told the CBC, "If
people are writing music

00:06:20.307 --> 00:06:22.168
"for that particular format,
then I find that sad."

00:06:22.168 --> 00:06:24.310
And she would know sad.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.239
Check out our sad song episode.

00:06:26.239 --> 00:06:28.550
(LA singing "Angel" by Sarah McLachlan)

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:30.260
- But could the opposite be true?

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:32.340
Is it possible that Spotify's incentives

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:34.400
are merely tweaking what's always been

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:35.890
a heavily commercialized art form?

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:39.740
- I happen to like pop
music, he likes pop music,

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:43.020
so we make, probably, together
somewhat accessible music,

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:45.510
that has a home on some
of these platforms.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.950
I think you have to write good songs,

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:50.790
but I don't think you
can think too heavily on,

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.420
this is gonna work great on Spotify.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.497
- Is there a Spotify
sound in your opinion?

00:06:55.497 --> 00:06:58.181
- There is the Spotify sound,

00:06:58.181 --> 00:07:01.860
and for a while there is was mumble rap.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:05.780
- I think that Spotify Song
is ever-changing, probably,

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:07.340
from season to season.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:08.620
- LA and I are going to write a song

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.730
with all of the elements Spotify loves,

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:13.770
and hopefully it ends up
with a billion streams.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.730
Well, what do you think about Spotify?

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:17.787
- I think it definitely has changed

00:07:17.787 --> 00:07:20.377
how music is made, how we listen to music,

00:07:20.377 --> 00:07:23.830
and I think we should play with,
in the song that we create,

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:25.810
is the length of it, length of the song.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.227
I think it should be a 30 second song.

00:07:28.227 --> 00:07:29.693
A 30 second song. (laughing)

00:07:29.693 --> 00:07:31.810
- So, typical "Sound Field" fashion.

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:35.420
I think we should be appealing to Gen Z.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.497
- And they're streaming natives.

00:07:37.497 --> 00:07:38.570
- They're streaming a lot.

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:41.110
- We are streaming immigrants,

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:44.370
like we grew up with other things.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:45.203
- We'll do it.

00:07:45.203 --> 00:07:46.036
- [LA] Let's get it.

00:07:46.036 --> 00:07:48.000
Before we hear our Spotify-style track,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.290
we'd like to thank Audible
for supporting PBS.

